WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:10.800
Looking at the next guideline, we have three plan options: on the left as

2
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:19.100
radio buttons, on the right as a dropdown. Which one is better in this case?

3
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:30.250
When we have few options like this example, it's always better to use radio buttons instead of dropdowns, so all the options are visible and I

4
00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:41.400
can see them all the time without opening a dropdown menu. But if I don't have enough space for radio buttons, say I'm on mobile with no

5
00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:52.550
room to stack them, then I'll use a dropdown. That violates the guideline, but I have a reason. These guidelines are the best default, but sometimes

6
00:00:52.550 --> 00:01:03.700
a reason appears to break one. It's still always better to follow the guidelines in general, even for simple components like the checkbox and the radio button.

7
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:14.525
The next guideline: do I leave a default selection in radio buttons or not? It's always better to have a default. Once a

8
00:01:14.525 --> 00:01:25.350
radio button has a choice, I can't go back to a state with nothing chosen; that state exists only in checkboxes. With radio

9
00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:36.175
buttons I always need one option selected. If I leave the user with nothing chosen, as soon as they pick one they can't

10
00:01:36.175 --> 00:01:47.000
return to that state, and if they try to continue with nothing chosen they get an error. So I need a default option.

11
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:59.100
It's also very important that any set of options I show the user is comprehensive: it covers everything that could come up. In this example, which isn't a real

12
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:11.200
one, if the user is creating an account and needs to choose their job, and my options don't cover every job and give no way to type one, they get

13
00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:23.300
stuck not knowing what to pick or write. So I need to give them an Others option or a way to type. The options must be as comprehensive as possible.

14
00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:34.940
Another important point is nested options, options inside each other, sometimes called a parent-child relationship: a radio button with other

15
00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:46.580
radio buttons beneath it. This is usually not done because it isn't understandable: if I pick the main radio, does it

16
00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:58.220
pick the two under it too? We said a radio can't select more than one option at a time, so

17
00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:09.860
this pattern makes no sense and can confuse users. Unlike a checkbox, which can have a parent-child relationship or a nested

18
00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:21.500
menu. Radio buttons can't, since I can't choose more than one: did I pick only the parent, or all the children?

19
00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:32.080
Let's pause the video and try to find the problem with using a radio or checkbox the way it's used here. The mistake

20
00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:42.660
is that we used it to trigger an action: tapping it turns on airplane mode. But radio buttons and checkboxes don't do that.

21
00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:53.240
They change a setting or make choices, and afterward I need to take an action for the choice to take effect. For example,

22
00:03:53.240 --> 00:04:03.820
if I choose my job and press Next, that's when the action runs. No action runs when I press a radio button or

23
00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:14.400
checkbox, except for the standalone checkbox we said can be used on desktop. Next we'll look at another pattern that runs the action instantly.

24
00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:24.250
Finally, the last guideline for checkboxes and radio buttons: make the clickable area the whole thing: the component itself and the label written next to

25
00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:34.100
it should all be clickable, not just the checkbox or just the radio. Otherwise it can reduce accessibility or make the component harder to use.
